full_model_response:
For the real symmetric case, diagonalizing \(H\) gives the Jacobian factor \(|E_1-E_2|\), so the eigenvalues repel linearly. Integrating over the center-of-mass energy gives the spacing law; rescaling by the mean spacing gives the conventional Wigner surmise.

\[
\boxed{
\begin{gathered}
\text{(a) } P(E_1,E_2)=\frac{1}{4\sqrt{\pi}}\,|E_1-E_2|\,
e^{-(E_1^2+E_2^2)/2},\qquad E_1,E_2\in\mathbb R.\\
\text{Equivalently, if ordered }E_1<E_2,\text{ multiply this by }2.\\[2mm]
\text{(b) For the raw splitting }s=|E_1-E_2|:\quad
P(s)=\frac{s}{2}e^{-s^2/4},\quad s\ge 0,
\text{ so }c_1=\frac12,\ c_2=\frac14.\\
\text{With unit mean spacing }x=s/\sqrt{\pi}:\quad
P(x)=\frac{\pi}{2}x e^{-\pi x^2/4},
\text{ the standard Wigner surmise.}\\[2mm]
\text{(c) For complex Hermitian } \hat H,\text{ the small-spacing behavior is }
P(s)\propto s^2\quad(s\to0),
\text{ i.e. quadratic level repulsion.}
\end{gathered}
}
\]

